    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a10758ff_678a4a0a",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/DnsResolver.java",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 200,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-15T12:33:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes I tend to prefer a version where we do pass the query. It tends to make things much easier for developers to use.\n\nDoing this in another patch is absolutely fine.\n\nAs for gathering the arguments... it\u0027s a difficult call. I do think than Junyu\u0027s proposal is cleaner, but I wonder if YAGNI. If we go that road then we need a different object for the query with blob and the query with string.\n\nBut yes, I think it\u0027s probably a good idea to implement with Junyu\u0027s suggestion.\n\nHow about something like this :\n\n    public interface AnswerParser\u003cT\u003e {\n        @NonNull T parse(@NonNull byte[] rawAnswer) throws ParseException;\n    }\n\n    public static abstract class AnswerCallback\u003cQueryType extends Query, AnswerType\u003e {\n        // Just checked, this actually works as expected. Surprisingly.\n        public final AnswerParser\u003c? extends AnswerType\u003e parser;\n        public AnswerCallback(AnswerParser\u003c? extends AnswerType\u003e p) { this.parser \u003d p; }\n        public abstract void onAnswer(@NonNull QueryType query, @NonNull AnswerType answer);\n        public abstract void onParseException(@NonNull ParseException exception);\n        public abstract void onQueryException(@NonNull ErrnoException exception);\n    }\n\n    public abstract static class RawAnswerCallback\u003cQueryType extends Query\u003e extends AnswerCallback\u003cQueryType, byte[]\u003e {\n        public RawAnswerCallback() {\n            super(rawAnswer -\u003e rawAnswer);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public abstract static class InetAddressAnswerCallback\u003cQueryType extends Query\u003e extends AnswerCallback\u003cQueryType, List\u003cInetAddress\u003e\u003e {\n        public InetAddressAnswerCallback() {\n            super(rawAnswer -\u003e new ArrayList\u003cInetAddress\u003e());\n        }\n    }\n\n    public \u003cT\u003e void query(RawQuery query, AnswerCallback\u003c? super RawQuery, T\u003e callback) {}\n    public \u003cT\u003e void query(DomainQuery query, AnswerCallback\u003c? super DomainQuery, T\u003e callback) {}\n\n\nWhat\u0027s nice about this is : you can reuse much better, you are sure to get the right answer, and everything is statically checked. You can pass a RawAnwserCallback\u003cDomainQuery\u003e with a domain query if you want to do that, you can pass a InetAddressAnswerCallback\u003cRawQuery\u003e if you want to formulate the query yourself but want to leave it to the platform to parse the answer, you can write a parser that returns an ArrayList and another that returns a LinkedList, but both can be handled by the same callback that takes a List.\n\nThe drawback is that the type argument to RawAnswerCallback and InetAddressAnswerCallback can\u0027t be inferred by the compiler, so you have to write it explicitly, which is redundant. I think it\u0027s still better.",
      "parentUuid": "7eeaa03d_b096dc95",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 200,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 200,
        "endChar": 21
      },
